    cons: [
      "Heavy and poorly balanced compared with professional machines",
      "Dust collection through the supplied bag is weak; a vacuum is essential",
      "Build quality is basic and vibration is noticeable",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Gets a renovation done for very little money. Connect a real vacuum and accept the weight.",
    description:
      "A powered drywall sander is a tool most people need exactly once, during a renovation, after which it goes into a cupboard. Buying professional equipment for that is difficult to justify, and this machine exists precisely for the one-project buyer. It has the essential features — full length reach, variable speed, an articulating head and a dust port — at a price that competes with renting for a week. On our full room test it sanded the compound down properly and produced an acceptable finish, which is the whole requirement. The compromises are weight, balance and dust. It is heavy, and the balance places more of that weight further from your hands than the better machines do, so fatigue arrives sooner. The supplied dust bag is close to useless: in our weighing it let a large fraction escape, and using it means a house full of compound dust regardless of the machine being powered. Connecting a shop vacuum instead transforms the result and should be treated as mandatory rather than optional. Vibration is higher than professional machines and the fit and finish is basic throughout. None of that stops it doing the job. For one renovation, it is a rational purchase; for repeated professional use it would not survive.",
  },
  {
