    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive relative to mid range machines",
      "Heavy, which is the price of the motor",
      "Requires a good vacuum to reach its extraction potential",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one that does not slow down. For heavy compound and long days, the extra power is not wasted.",
    description:
      "Most drywall sanding is light work in the sense that joint compound is soft, but there is a specific situation where power matters: a heavily built up joint, a textured ceiling being flattened, or old compound that has fully cured and hardened. Underpowered machines slow noticeably in that material and the sanding disc loads up as a result. The DEWALT has the strongest motor here and it simply does not bog, holding speed through material that made the budget machines labour. On our ceiling section, where the compound was thickest, it was appreciably faster. Construction matches the motor. The housing and extension are built to survive job site treatment rather than careful workshop handling, the grips are positioned sensibly for a tool held at an angle for long periods, and the speed control has a genuinely usable low setting for delicate work near an edge. Extraction is effective with a suitable vacuum connected. The costs are money and mass. It sits well above mid range machines on price, and the motor that makes it capable also makes it heavy, which over a full day of ceilings is felt. Like every machine here except the Festool, it needs a good vacuum to achieve acceptable dust capture.",
  },
  {
    id: "radius-360-hand-sander",
    name: "Radius 360 Air Hand Sander",
    category: "Hand Held, 9 in Round, Swivel Head, Vacuum Port",
    badge: "Best Hand Sander",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 6300,
