      "Round shape with no corners to dig into the surface",
      "Vacuum port available, which is rare on a hand sander",
      "Very light and precise for detail work and small repairs",
      "Inexpensive compared with any powered machine",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Manual sanding is slow across a whole room",
      "Requires physical effort, particularly on ceilings",
      "Round shape cannot reach fully into corners",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Not a replacement for a powered sander, but the best hand tool here and the right choice for patch work.",
    description:
      "Powered drywall sanders make sense for a room or a house. For a patch around a new light fitting, a repaired doorway, or touching up a few joints, dragging out a long machine and a vacuum is disproportionate, and hand sanding is both faster overall and more controllable. The Radius 360 is the best hand tool in this category and the reason is the swivel head. A conventional hand sanding block is held at whatever angle your wrist happens to be, which means the edge digs in and leaves marks. The swivel keeps the disc flat against the wall no matter how you hold the handle, and the round shape has no corners to gouge with. The result is a noticeably better finish than a flat block produces, particularly for someone without much practice. A vacuum port is available, which is unusual on a hand sander and makes a real difference to the dust in a small repair job where sheeting the room off is not worth it. It attaches to a pole for ceilings. What it cannot do is compete on speed. Sanding a full room by hand is slow and physically demanding work, especially overhead, and that is exactly what powered machines exist to solve. As a companion to one, or as the only tool for small work, it is excellent.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Drywall Sander",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-drywall-sander/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best drywall sander?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Festool PLANEX LHS 2 for professionals, because its dust extraction is far ahead of anything else and it is the only machine that keeps a room usable while sanding. For most people the Porter-Cable 7800 is the sensible choice, and the WEN 6369 handles a single renovation cheaply.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a vacuum with a drywall sander?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, on every machine except the Festool the supplied bag captures only a fraction of the dust. You also need the right vacuum: a standard filter passes compound dust straight through and exhausts it into the room, so a fine dust or HEPA filter is essential rather than optional.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What grit should I use for drywall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Between 120 and 150 grit for finish sanding. Coarser cuts through the compound too quickly and leaves scratches that show through paint, while finer clogs almost immediately on soft compound. Use 100 grit only for knocking down heavy ridges before finish sanding.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use a random orbit sander on drywall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can, but it is slow and risky. The small pad means many more passes, there is no reach for ceilings, and the aggressive cut makes it easy to go through the compound into the paper face of the board, which then needs re-mudding and re-sanding.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I stop drywall dust getting everywhere?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Extraction at the tool with a fine filtered vacuum does most of it. Then seal doorways with taped plastic rather than hung sheets, cover HVAC vents and switch the system off, and put a fan in a window blowing outward to keep the work room at negative pressure.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is sanding drywall dust dangerous?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It should be treated seriously. The particles are in the respirable size range and many joint compounds contain crystalline silica. A rated half mask respirator, not a paper nuisance mask, plus sealed eye protection is the correct precaution for any extended sanding.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: "2026-08-24",
      dateModified: "2026-08-24",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-24",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-24",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools",
    tags: [
      "drywall sander",
      "pole sander",
      "joint compound",
      "drywall finishing",
      "dust extraction",
      "renovation",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
