        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sanding drywall by hand is slow, and sanding it with a power sander without proper extraction fills a house with an extraordinarily fine dust that finds its way into everything for weeks. A good drywall sander solves both problems at once, and how well it captures dust matters considerably more than how fast it sands.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: The Dust Problem Is the Whole Problem -->
        <div id="dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Dust Problem Is the Whole Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Joint compound dust behaves differently from sawdust, and understanding why explains everything about how these tools are designed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Is Extraordinarily Fine</h3>

          <p>
            Sanded compound produces particles far smaller than wood dust, light enough to remain airborne for hours and to travel through a house on the smallest air current. It settles on every surface in every room, including ones with the door closed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ordinary Vacuums Redistribute It</h3>

          <p>
            A shop vacuum with standard filtration captures the coarse fraction and blows the fine fraction straight back into the room through the exhaust. Without a fine dust or HEPA filter, using a vacuum can make the airborne situation worse rather than better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Gets Into Everything Mechanical</h3>

          <p>
            Compound dust is mildly abrasive and finds its way into computer fans, appliance vents, door hinges and tool bearings. Damage from a badly managed drywall sanding job shows up weeks later in unrelated equipment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silica Content Makes It a Health Matter</h3>

          <p>
            Many joint compounds contain crystalline silica, and the dust from sanding is in the respirable size range. This is not merely a nuisance, and a rated respirator rather than a paper mask is the correct precaution.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extraction Beats Cleanup Every Time</h3>

          <p>
            A machine that captures ninety percent of the dust at the head saves hours of cleaning and keeps the rest of the house usable. This is why extraction performance, not sanding speed, is the specification that should decide the purchase.
          </p>

