        <!-- SECTION 2: Pole Sanders, Powered Sanders and Hand Tools -->
        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Pole Sanders, Powered Sanders and Hand Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three quite different tools share this name, and the right one depends entirely on the size of the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Manual pole sanders are cheap and slow.</strong> A sanding head on a swivel at the end of a pole, with a screen or paper attached. They cost very little, need no power, and sanding a whole room with one is genuinely hard work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Powered long reach sanders are the room-sized answer.</strong> A rotating disc on the end of an extension with dust extraction built in. They turn a two day hand sanding job into a few hours, and they are what every professional uses.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hand held drywall sanders handle detail and patches.</strong> Round swivel-head tools for small repairs, edges and corners. Every job needs some of this work regardless of what powered machine you own.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Random orbit sanders are a poor substitute.</strong> They can sand compound, but the small pad, the lack of reach, and the aggressive cut make it slow and easy to dig through the paper face of the board.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wet sanding is the no-dust alternative.</strong> A damp sponge dissolves and smooths compound rather than abrading it, producing no airborne dust at all. It is slower, it leaves a slightly softer surface, and it cannot handle heavy build-up, but in an occupied house with no way to isolate the room it is often the correct choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Corner tools exist and are worth having.</strong> Inside corners are where most amateur drywall work betrays itself, because no round sander reaches fully into them. An angled corner sanding tool costs very little and finishes the one area that is visible from everywhere in the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Most jobs need two tools.</strong> A powered machine for the flats and a hand sander for corners, edges and around fittings. Buying only the powered machine leaves you improvising in exactly the places where the finish is most visible.
          </p>
        </div>

