        <!-- SECTION 4: Sanding Compound Without Ruining It -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sanding Compound Without Ruining It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drywall sanding is unusual in that the most common error is doing too much rather than too little, and the damage is not always visible until the paint goes on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a light touch and let the disc work.</strong> Joint compound is soft and a powered sander removes it fast. Pressing produces swirl marks and cuts through the compound into the paper face of the board, which then has to be re-mudded.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never sand through to the paper.</strong> Once the paper face is fuzzed, it will show through paint as a rough patch and it absorbs primer differently. Repairing it means another coat of compound and another sanding session.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the head moving in overlapping circles.</strong> A stationary rotating disc dishes the compound. Continuous overlapping movement across the joint blends the edges into the board surface, which is the whole objective.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Work 120 to 150 grit.</strong> Coarser cuts through compound too fast and leaves scratches that show. Finer clogs immediately on soft compound. This range is what professionals use for finish sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Feather the edges rather than sanding the middle.</strong> A visible joint is usually a ridge at the edge of the compound, not a lump in the centre. Concentrating on blending the perimeter into the board does more for the finish than flattening the middle.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check with raking light before you stop.</strong> Hold a work light flat against the wall and look along the surface. Ridges and hollows invisible under room lighting appear immediately, and finding them now is far cheaper than finding them after painting.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Prime Before You Judge It</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Sanded compound and drywall paper look completely different under paint because they absorb it at different rates. A wall that looks perfect bare will show every joint once painted. A coat of primer reveals the true state of the surface while it is still easy to touch up, and it is the step that separates professional-looking work from obviously amateur work.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Choosing the Vacuum, Not Just the Sander -->
        <div id="vacuum" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Choosing the Vacuum, Not Just the Sander
          </h2>

          <p>
            On every machine here except one, the dust performance is determined by the vacuum you attach rather than by the sander itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The supplied bag is never enough.</strong> Onboard collection bags capture a fraction of the dust at best. Every review of these tools that praises dust collection is describing performance with a vacuum attached, and the bag alone gives a very different experience.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fine dust filtration is the specification that matters.</strong> A standard shop vacuum filter passes compound dust straight through and exhausts it into the room. A HEPA or fine dust filter, plus a fine dust collection bag, is what actually captures it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Suction volume matters more than peak pressure.</strong> Drywall sanding produces a high volume of light dust rather than heavy debris, so airflow through the hose is what carries it away. A vacuum rated for airflow rather than lift performs better.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Autostart is genuinely useful.</strong> Vacuums that switch on when the tool is triggered remove the temptation to make one quick pass without turning the extraction on, which is how a room gets dusted.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expect to change the bag often.</strong> Compound dust fills a vacuum bag quickly and a full bag chokes airflow, at which point extraction quietly stops working while the machine sounds exactly the same. Check it far more often than seems necessary.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Budget for it before buying the sander.</strong> Adding a proper vacuum to a cheap sander produces better real-world results than a better sander with a poor vacuum, so if the budget is fixed, that is where the money should go.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Timing, Coats and Knowing When to Stop -->
        <div id="when-to-sand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Timing, Coats and Knowing When to Stop
          </h2>

          <p>
            Half of a good drywall finish is decided before any sanding happens, in how the compound was applied and how long it was left.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Compound must be fully dry, not merely surface dry.</strong> Sanding compound that is still damp underneath tears rather than cuts, loads the disc instantly, and leaves a scarred surface that needs recoating. In a cool or humid room, overnight is a minimum and longer is safer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Three coats is the normal expectation.</strong> A tape coat, a fill coat and a finish coat, each wider than the last. Trying to achieve the finish in two coats means each is thicker, dries slower, shrinks more, and needs far more sanding to flatten.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sand lightly between coats, not thoroughly.</strong> Between coats the job is knocking down ridges and lumps so the next coat sits flat, which takes a few passes. Full sanding at this stage wastes effort because the next coat covers it anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Better application means dramatically less sanding.</strong> A well feathered coat applied with a wide knife needs a fraction of the sanding that a thick, ridged coat does. Time spent getting the compound flat with the knife is repaid several times over at the sanding stage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Shrinkage shows up after drying.</strong> Compound loses volume as it dries, so a joint that looked filled can show a slight hollow the following morning. Checking after full drying and adding a thin skim where needed is normal rather than a failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stop when raking light shows nothing.</strong> The temptation is to keep going for a perfect surface, and past a certain point more sanding only risks breaking through to the paper. When a light held flat against the wall casts no shadows, the joint is finished.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Preparing the Room -->
        <div id="preparation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Preparing the Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            Even excellent extraction leaves some dust, and half an hour of preparation saves a week of finding compound in unexpected places.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Seal doorways with plastic and tape.</strong> Not a hung sheet, which billows and lets dust past, but plastic taped along all four edges with a zipped or overlapped access slit. Fine dust travels through the smallest gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cover or seal air vents and returns.</strong> A running HVAC system will distribute compound dust through the entire house and load the filter and ductwork. Tape over supply and return grilles in the work area, and switch the system off while sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Remove what you can, cover what you cannot.</strong> Anything with a fan, a vent or a moving part suffers from compound dust. Electronics, appliances and tools should leave the room rather than be covered.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Open a window with a fan blowing out.</strong> Negative pressure in the work room keeps dust from migrating into the rest of the house and gives the fine airborne fraction somewhere to go.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wear a proper respirator and eye protection.</strong> Compound dust in the respirable range with silica content is not something to breathe, and it is deeply unpleasant in the eyes. A rated half mask and sealed goggles are the correct kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Vacuum, then damp wipe, then vacuum again.</strong> Sweeping puts the dust back into the air. Vacuum with a fine filter first, wipe surfaces with a damp cloth to capture what remains, then vacuum once more after everything has settled overnight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best drywall sander?</h3>
