              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a vacuum with a drywall sander?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on every machine except the Festool the supplied bag captures only a fraction of the dust. You also need the right vacuum: a standard filter passes compound dust straight through and exhausts it into the room, so a fine dust or HEPA filter is essential rather than optional.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What grit should I use for drywall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Between 120 and 150 grit for finish sanding. Coarser cuts through the compound too quickly and leaves scratches that show through paint, while finer clogs almost immediately on soft compound. Use 100 grit only for knocking down heavy ridges before finish sanding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a random orbit sander on drywall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but it is slow and risky. The small pad means many more passes, there is no reach for ceilings, and the aggressive cut makes it easy to go through the compound into the paper face of the board, which then needs re-mudding and re-sanding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop drywall dust getting everywhere?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Extraction at the tool with a fine filtered vacuum does most of it. Then seal doorways with taped plastic rather than hung sheets, cover HVAC vents and switch the system off, and put a fan in a window blowing outward to keep the work room at negative pressure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is sanding drywall dust dangerous?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It should be treated seriously. The particles are in the respirable size range and many joint compounds contain crystalline silica. A rated half mask respirator, not a paper nuisance mask, plus sealed eye protection is the correct precaution for any extended sanding.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Drywall Sanding Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drywall sanding errors either show up under paint or fill your house with dust, and both are avoidable.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding Through to the Paper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Joint compound is soft and a powered sander cuts through it fast. Once the paper face of the board is fuzzed it absorbs paint differently and shows as a rough patch, which means another coat of compound and another sanding session. Use a light touch and let the disc do the work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the Dust Bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every machine here except the Festool captures only a fraction of the dust through its bag, and compound dust is fine enough to stay airborne for hours and reach every room. Connect a vacuum, and make sure that vacuum has a fine dust or HEPA filter rather than a standard one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the Wall Before Priming</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bare compound and drywall paper absorb paint at different rates, so a wall that looks flawless unpainted will show every joint once the colour goes on. A coat of primer reveals the true surface while touch-ups are still easy, and skipping it is the classic amateur error.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the HVAC Running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A running heating or cooling system pulls compound dust into the return, distributes it through every room in the house, and loads both the filter and the ductwork. Tape over the grilles in the work area and switch the system off before you start sanding.
              </p>
            </div>
